Description: Take the main executable's path from the system in CppInterOp
 CppInterOp's GetExecutablePath() used the dladdr() file name of a symbol in
 the main executable. For the main executable that is argv[0] as typed, so
 `jank` started through PATH from a directory containing any entry named
 `jank` made initializeDyld() open that entry as an object file; cantFail()
 then failed and jank crashed with SIGSEGV. Use
 llvm::sys::fs::getMainExecutable(), which reads /proc/self/exe on Linux.
Author: Adam Kalisz <adam.kalisz@notnullmakers.com>
Forwarded: not-yet
Last-Update: 2026-11-05
---
--- a/compiler+runtime/third-party/cppinterop/lib/CppInterOp/DynamicLibraryManagerSymbol.cpp	2026-11-01 18:51:11.000000000 +0000
+++ b/compiler+runtime/third-party/cppinterop/lib/CppInterOp/DynamicLibraryManagerSymbol.cpp	2026-11-05 13:15:48.836845120 +0000
@@ -467,9 +467,13 @@
 // address of main, and some platforms can't implement GetMainExecutable
 // without being given the address of a function in the main executable).
 std::string GetExecutablePath() {
-  // This just needs to be some symbol in the binary; C++ doesn't
-  // allow taking the address of ::main however.
-  return DynamicLibraryManager::getSymbolLocation(&GetExecutablePath);
+  // getSymbolLocation() reports the dladdr() file name, which for the main
+  // executable is argv[0] as typed (e.g. "jank"). Resolved against the current
+  // directory, that can name an unrelated file, and opening it as an object
+  // file then fails in initializeDyld(). getMainExecutable() asks the system
+  // (/proc/self/exe on Linux); the function address is the fallback hint.
+  return llvm::sys::fs::getMainExecutable(
+      nullptr, reinterpret_cast<void*>(&GetExecutablePath));
 }
 
 class Dyld {
